                                                "sense_id": "81d4f328-704d-36d8-8bc9-1289d4df7dab",
                                                "text": "loaf about, loaf around"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "84fbb633-8e18-487a-a458-b3fd22f01e55",
                                                "sense_id": "81d4f328-704d-36d8-8bc9-1289d4df7dab",
                                                "text": "2015, Elizabeth Royte, Vultures Are Revolting. Here’s Why We Need to Save Them., National Geographic (December 2015)https://web.archive.org/web/20151213095110/http://ngm.nationalgeographic.com/2016/01/vultures-text\nThey don’t (often) kill other animals, they probably form monogamous pairs, and we know they share parental care of chicks, and loaf and bathe in large, congenial groups."
                                            }
